Accurate clock reasoning must model continuous movement.

## 26. Second-Hand Reasoning

The second hand moves $360° / 60 = 6°$ per second. Once seconds are included, the minute and hour hands are no longer static between whole minutes either:

@math name="Minute Hand (with seconds)" label="eq:minute-angle-sec"
\theta_m = 6M + 0.1S
@/math

@math name="Hour Hand (with seconds)" label="eq:hour-angle-sec"
\theta_h = 30H + 0.5M + \frac{S}{120}
@/math

This allows precise modeling of all three hands.

## 27. Finding When Two Hands Overlap

The minute hand moves at $6°$/minute and the hour hand at $0.5°$/minute — a relative speed of $6 - 0.5 = 5.5°$/minute. For the minute hand to lap the hour hand (gain a full $360°$ on it) takes:

@math label="eq:overlap-time"
\frac{360}{5.5} = \frac{720}{11} = 65\frac{5}{11}
@/math

minutes, i.e. roughly **65.45 minutes** — about 1 hour, 5 minutes, 27 seconds. So the hands overlap approximately every 65.45 minutes, not every 60.

